Reverse proxy server

A reverse proxy acts as a "middleman" between the internet and your backend servers. When a user visits your site, they talk to the proxy,
and the proxy talks to your application.

How It Works
Request: User sends a request to example.com.
Intercept: The reverse proxy (e.g., Nginx) receives the request.
Forward: The proxy passes the request to the correct internal container.
Respond: The proxy sends the app's response back to the user.

Why Use One?
Security: Hides your backend server's IP and protects against direct attacks.
SSL Termination: One place to manage HTTPS certificates instead of in every app.
Load Balancing: Spreads traffic across multiple instances of your container.
Caching: Stores static files to serve them faster and reduce server load.
URL Masking: Makes localhost:8080 look like a clean ://example.com URL.

Example Architecture
In a Docker setup, you typically have:
External Network: Only the Reverse Proxy is exposed to the internet.
Internal Network: Your Database, API, and Frontend talk to each other privately.

